--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT SHIPPED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A complete, HIG-native SwiftUI SpeedProfilePage at web parity: a pure @Observable state holder
(ADR-004 — no networking in the view) over an injectable SpeedProfileDataSource seam whose methods keep
the web hook names at the Swift call sites (loadVehicles ← useSelectedVehicle/GET /vehicles,
useSpeedProfile ← GET /analytics/speed-profile?vehicle_id&start&end, useDrives ← GET /drives?vehicle_id).
The web per-drive efficiency heuristic (getEfficiency: prefers measured energyUsedWh/km, else the
battery-draw fallback (battUsed * 0.75 * 1000) / km Wh/km) and every derivation (the `drives` date
window, `scatterData`, `bucketEfficiency`, the per-bucket detail cards — the web useMemo blocks) are
ported verbatim into the deterministic, harness-verified SpeedProfileEngine. Unit conversion is
injected into the engine as closures so the bucketing/aggregation is pure + testable; the production
view builds those closures from the shared KMP Units facade (P1/S5) at the render boundary only
(ADR-005). SpeedProfileData carries SI fields (avgSpeedMps / peakSpeedMps / optimalSpeedMps) and the
distribution band labels verbatim; the bucket tier/icon ladders (web bucketColor / bucketTextClass /
categoryIcon) and the scatter efficiency tier (web color ternary) are ported as colorblind-safe
brand-palette slots (TSChartPalette) + semantic TSTone, never hardcoded hex.

The page reproduces the web chrome (PageContainer title + subtitle + the global VehicleSelect + the
RangePicker date filter, default preset "all" → 2015-01-01..today) and ALL of the web's sections:

  • GlassPanel1 — the hero average / peak / optimal speed RadialGauges (each trimmed to its web `max`
    in SI: 200 km/h avg & optimal, 250 km/h peak), tinted cyan / red / green, value+unit shown inside.
  • Speed-Distribution — the speed-bucket histogram (ChartContainer + BarChart, one band-tier-colored
    bar per band, height = reading count; web `pct: b.readings`), with a band-label axis + CSV export.
  • GlassPanel3 — the per-band detail cards (category glyph + label, time-share % tier-tinted, drive
    count, and — when drives fell into the band — averaged speed + efficiency tier-tinted).
  • Efficiency-vs-Speed — the per-drive efficiency-vs-speed scatter (ChartContainer + ScatterChart,
    one series per efficiency tier so points carry the web Cell colors), with the "Lower {unit} =
    better" subtitle, the Speed/efficiency axis legend, and the Efficient/Moderate/High-consumption
    color legend.
  • GlassPanel5 — the green-accented efficiency insight naming the optimal speed.

Every data state the source produces is implemented and never collapses the chrome: `loading` →
SpeedProfileSkeleton under SwiftUI `.redacted(.placeholder)`; `error` (web useSpeedProfile error into
PageContainer) → TSErrorDisplay + Retry; `empty` → the web single `noData` TSEmptyState when the
roll-up is absent (web `data ? … : EmptyState`) PLUS each panel's own empty (distribution empty,
scatter ≤3 points = web `scatterData.length > 3`, detail-cards empty, insight when optimal ≤ 0);
`success` → the full populated layout. useDrives degrades silently to an empty drives list on failure
(web has no error surface for it), so the scatter/table fall back to their own empties without blocking
the page.

Adaptive (ADR-002/006): the hero gauge grid, the detail-card grid, and the chart panels reflow for
macOS / iPad regular width vs. compact iPhone via `#if os(iOS)` horizontalSizeClass (macOS = regular).
The page is registered for a NEW `.speedProfile` AppRoute (web /speed-profile resolves to it via the
canonical path segment "speed-profile"; grouped under .vehicle alongside .driving/.efficiency) and
hosted through SpeedProfileRouteRegistration in TeslaSyncApp — mirroring exactly how the sibling
`.efficiency` route was added (the `.driving` route is already owned by DriveScore, so SpeedProfile
needs its own route). All 21 visible strings resolve from Localizable.xcstrings with the web key names;
SI units render via Units + SpeedProfilePageFormat at the boundary only (a11y labels/values on every
gauge + chart; VoiceOver, Dynamic Type, light/dark honored).

Files (apps/apple/Sources/Features/Driving/, mirroring the EfficiencyPage split):
